10. MongoDB Sharding Demonstration
// On mongos (after setting up config servers + shard servers)
sh.enableSharding("CollegeDB")

// Sample dataset (≥20 records)
use CollegeDB
for (let i = 1; i <= 25; i++) {
  db.Students.insertOne({
    Student_ID: 100 + i,
    Name: "Student" + i,
    Course: i % 2 === 0 ? "BCA" : "MCA",
    Marks: 60 + (i % 40)
  })
}

// Choose shard key
sh.shardCollection("CollegeDB.Students", { Student_ID: 1 })

// Verify distribution
db.Students.getShardDistribution()

// Benefit: Horizontal scaling – data and load spread across multiple machines
11. Master-Slave (Primary-Secondary) Replication (MongoDB)
# Primary (port 27017)
mongod --replSet rs0 --port 27017 --dbpath /data/primary

# Secondary (port 27018)
mongod --replSet rs0 --port 27018 --dbpath /data/secondary

# Initiate replica set
mongosh --port 27017
rs.initiate({
  _id: "rs0",
  members: [
    { _id: 0, host: "localhost:27017" },
    { _id: 1, host: "localhost:27018" }
  ]
})

# Insert on primary
use CollegeDB
db.Students.insertOne({ Student_ID: 201, Name: "Test", Course: "BCA", Marks: 80 })

# Verify on secondary
mongosh --port 27018
rs.secondaryOk()
db.Students.find()

# When primary becomes unavailable → secondary is elected as new primary (automatic failover)
12. Peer-to-Peer Replication (Cassandra)
# Start multiple nodes (node1, node2, node3)
# On each node configure cassandra.yaml (seeds, listen_address, etc.)

# Create keyspace with RF=3
CREATE KEYSPACE CollegeDB
WITH replication = {'class': 'NetworkTopologyStrategy', 'datacenter1': 3};

USE CollegeDB;
CREATE TABLE Students (...);   -- same as Q5
INSERT INTO Students ...;

# Verify data on every node
SELECT * FROM Students;

# Peer-to-peer differs from master-slave:
# - Every node is equal (no single master)
# - Writes can go to any node
# - Higher availability & write scalability
# - Eventual consistency by default
13. Sharding + Replication Combined Architecture
Create multiple shards (each shard is itself a replica set).
Configure each shard as a 3-node replica set.
Insert data → mongos routes by shard key.
Verify distribution: db.collection.getShardDistribution()
Verify replica availability: kill one secondary → data still available from other replicas.
Benefit: Scalability (sharding) + Fault tolerance (replication).
14. MapReduce on Student Dataset (MongoDB)
use CollegeDB

// Sample data already present from earlier questions

// Map function
var map = function() {
  emit(this.Course, { count: 1, totalMarks: this.Marks });
};

// Reduce function
var reduce = function(key, values) {
  var result = { count: 0, totalMarks: 0 };
  values.forEach(function(v) {
    result.count += v.count;
    result.totalMarks += v.totalMarks;
  });
  return result;
};

// Finalize to calculate average
var finalize = function(key, reduced) {
  reduced.avgMarks = reduced.totalMarks / reduced.count;
  return reduced;
};

db.Students.mapReduce(map, reduce, {
  out: "CourseStats",
  finalize: finalize
})

db.CourseStats.find().pretty()
